    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#combined -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#combined">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/struct"/>
        <rdfs:label>combined (AlgorithmCompositionMode)</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>The included algorithm&apos;s update rules are merged into the outer loop and applied ONCE per outer iteration (1:1). Use when both algorithms update at the same cadence on the same observations. This is the default.</skos:definition>
        <skos:notation>combined</skos:notation>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#nested -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode#nested">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/AlgorithmCompositionMode"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/struct"/>
        <rdfs:label>nested (AlgorithmCompositionMode)</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>The included algorithm runs as a full inner loop on EACH outer iteration, re-converging before the outer update rules are applied. Use when the inner algorithm maintains an invariant the outer one would otherwise perturb — e.g. FIC holding the E-I working point (mean S_e = 0.25) while EIB retunes per-edge coupling. The outer update&apos;s validity depends on that invariant, so the inner loop must re-settle it between every outer step.</skos:definition>
        <skos:notation>nested</skos:notation>
    </owl:Class>
    


