    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/HemodynamicWarmupRequirement -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/HemodynamicWarmupRequirement">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Modelling axiom: for any tvbo:HemodynamicObservation, the discarded transient (tvbo:transient_time) MUST be ≥ the observation&apos;s HRF kernel duration (tvbo:hrfKernelDuration). Otherwise the causal HRF convolution is under-filled and the BOLD time axis shifts earlier, so the signal spuriously precedes the stimulus rather than lagging it by the hemodynamic delay. Example: the tvboptim BOLD pipeline uses a 20000 ms kernel (5000 samples at dt = 4 ms) via prepend_history + valid-mode convolution, so transient_time ≥ 20000 ms is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Hemodynamic warm-up requirement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/HemodynamicObservation"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/hrfKernelDuration"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/transient_time"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hemodynamic warm-up requirement</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Observed empirically (TVB-O acoustic-stimulation use-case, 2026): a 15000 ms transient (&lt; 20000 ms kernel) made auditory BOLD precede the loudness by ~1 s with a mislabelled 1043 ms TR; transient ≥ 20000 ms restored a causal hemodynamic lag (≈ +1.5 s) and the correct 1000 ms TR.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


